### What is a stub?

A stub is a fake that returns preset values to control what the code under test sees. You use it when you care about the result, not the interaction.

```java
// Stub: control what the code sees
package com.bank.service;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import static org.mockito.Mockito.*;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;

public class CurrencyConverterTest {

    @Test
    void convertsUsingStubRate() {
        // STUB: return a fixed rate regardless of how many times called
        ExchangeRateService rateService = mock(ExchangeRateService.class);
        when(rateService.getRate("USD", "INR")).thenReturn(new BigDecimal("83.50"));

        CurrencyConverter converter = new CurrencyConverter(rateService);
        BigDecimal result = converter.convert(
            new BigDecimal("1000.00"), "USD", "INR"
        );

        // Assert the RESULT (state-based)
        assertEquals(new BigDecimal("83500.00"), result);
        // We do NOT verify how many times getRate was called
    }
}
```

### What is a mock?

A mock records how it was called so you can verify interactions. You use it when the correct behavior IS the interaction.

```java
// Mock: verify interactions
@Test
void transferNotifiesCustomer() {
    // MOCK: record calls for later verification
    NotificationService notifier = mock(NotificationService.class);

    TransferService service = new TransferService(notifier);
    service.transfer("ACC-001", "ACC-002", new BigDecimal("5000.00"));

    // VERIFY the interaction (behavior-based)
    verify(notifier, times(1)).sendEmail(
        eq("rajesh@nationalbank.in"),
        contains("Transfer of Rs 5,000.00")
    );
}

@Test
void failedTransferDoesNotNotify() {
    NotificationService notifier = mock(NotificationService.class);
    TransferService service = new TransferService(notifier);

    assertThrows(InsufficientBalanceException.class, () ->
        service.transfer("ACC-001", "ACC-002", new BigDecimal("9999999"))
    );

    // VERIFY: notification was NOT sent on failure
    verify(notifier, never()).sendEmail(anyString(), anyString());
}
```

### What is the difference between a stub and a mock?

| Aspect | Stub | Mock |
|---|---|---|
| Purpose | Control behavior | Verify interaction |
| You configure | Return values | (also return values) |
| You assert | The result or state | The calls made |
| Question | "Given this input, is the output correct?" | "Did my code call the right thing?" |
| Example | Stub rate service to return 83.50 | Verify notification was sent once |

### How does Mockito work?

Mockito creates test doubles for Java classes and interfaces.

```java
// Mockito basics
import static org.mockito.Mockito.*;

// Create a mock
AccountRepository repo = mock(AccountRepository.class);

// Stub a method
when(repo.findById("ACC-001")).thenReturn(
    new Account("ACC-001", new BigDecimal("50000.00"))
);

// Stub an exception
when(repo.findById("INVALID")).thenThrow(
    new AccountNotFoundException("INVALID")
);

// Verify interactions
verify(repo).save(any(Account.class));
verify(repo, times(2)).findById(anyString());
verify(repo, never()).delete(anyString());

// Capture arguments
ArgumentCaptor<Account> captor = ArgumentCaptor.forClass(Account.class);
verify(repo).save(captor.capture());
assertEquals(new BigDecimal("45000.00"), captor.getValue().getBalance());
```

### How does WireMock work?

WireMock creates a fake HTTP server for mocking external REST APIs.

```java
// WireMock: mock an external fraud-check API
import com.github.tomakehurst.wiremock.client.WireMock;

@Test
void transferCallsFraudCheck() {
    // Stub the external API
    stubFor(post(urlEqualTo("/api/fraud-check"))
        .willReturn(aResponse()
            .withStatus(200)
            .withBody("{\"approved\": true}")
            .withHeader("Content-Type", "application/json")));

    // Run the code that calls the external API
    transferService.transfer("ACC-001", "ACC-002", new BigDecimal("50000"));

    // Verify the call was made with the right body
    WireMock.verify(postRequestedFor(urlEqualTo("/api/fraud-check"))
        .withRequestBody(containing("ACC-001")));
}

// Simulate a timeout
stubFor(post(urlEqualTo("/api/fraud-check"))
    .willReturn(aResponse()
        .withFixedDelay(30000))); // 30 second delay

// Simulate a server error
stubFor(post(urlEqualTo("/api/fraud-check"))
    .willReturn(aResponse().withStatus(500)));
```

### What other test doubles exist?

| Double | Description | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Dummy | Passed but never used | A null logger in a constructor |
| Stub | Returns preset values | Rate service returns 83.50 |
| Mock | Records and verifies calls | Notification service call count |
| Fake | Lightweight working implementation | In-memory repository |
| Spy | Wraps a real object, records calls | Real service with call tracking |

### When should you mock vs not mock?

Mock at the right boundary: mock external or slow collaborators, not the class under test.

```text
  DO mock:
  - External HTTP services (use WireMock)
  - Database in unit tests (use Mockito)
  - Email/notification services
  - Third-party APIs

  DO NOT mock:
  - The class you are testing
  - Simple value objects
  - Everything (over-mocking couples tests to implementation)
  - In integration tests (use the real thing)
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "When would you use a stub vs a mock?" Stub for state-based assertions (check the result). Mock when the interaction IS the behavior (verify the call).
- "What is over-mocking?" Mocking everything couples tests to implementation detail, making refactoring painful. Mock only the external boundary.
- "Can Mockito create stubs and mocks?" Yes. The same `mock()` object can both return stubbed values and be verified for interactions.
